  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 259 AM CDT Fri Jul 31 2026\n\nThunderstorms continue to push southeast across the forecast area \nthis morning as a potent shortwave trough advances east across the \nUpper Midwest into the Great Lakes. Storms have weakened over the \npast hour and this trend is expected to continue with storms \neventually moving out of the area by 6-7 AM. Rainfall rates of 1-2\" \nhave been observed, but the progression of storms will largely \nlimit any flooding concerns. A strong summer cold front will \nmove through the area this morning, with gusty northwest winds \nushering in cooler and drier air. Highs will top out in the mid \n80s along the KS/NE state line and in the low 90s south of I-35,\nwith dewpoints falling into the 60s behind the front. There is \na low chance (<15%) for a shower to form this afternoon on the \nwestern periphery of the closed upper low, but think most \nlocales stay dry.\n\nThe cooler and drier air becomes more noticeable this weekend. \nSurface ridging builds overhead for Saturday and breezy north winds \nwill keep temperatures 5-10 degrees below normal with highs in the \nlow to mid 80s. Low temperatures both Saturday and Sunday mornings \nwill be in the 60s, perhaps even the upper 50s! Southerly low-level \nwinds return on Sunday as the mid-level ridge oozes back over the \nPlains. Temperatures gradually warm each day into the middle of next \nweek with humidity returning as well. Ensembles favor more zonal \nflow over the Central Plains Wed-Fri, with embedded waves bringing \nthe next chance of precipitation.",
